+  <section class="journal" id="roasters-journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+    <div class="journal-header">
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <span class="journal-meta">From the roastery floor &middot; How our beans find their way to you</span>
+    </div>
+
+    <figure class="journal-figure">
+      <img
+        src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1447933601403-0c6688de566e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80"
+        alt="Freshly roasted coffee beans" loading="lazy">
+      <figcaption>Every lot is cupped, logged, and roasted in small batches.</figcaption>
+    </figure>
+
+    <div class="summary-controls">
+      <button type="button" class="btn" id="summarize-btn" aria-controls="journal-summary">Summarize</button>
+      <span class="summary-status" id="summary-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="summary-box" id="journal-summary" hidden>
+      <h3>Key Points</h3>
+      <ul id="summary-list"></ul>
+    </div>
+
+    <article class="journal-body" id="journal-text">
+      <p>
+        People sometimes ask us why a cup of coffee from The Daily Grind tastes the way it does. The honest answer
+        is that the flavor is decided long before the beans ever reach our roaster. It starts on hillsides in
+        Colombia, Ethiopia, Guatemala, and Sumatra, with farmers who have spent generations learning how altitude,
+        rainfall, and soil shape the character of a coffee cherry. Our job is mostly to find those people, earn
+        their trust, and then try not to get in the way of what they've already grown.
+      </p>
+
+      <h3>Finding the Right Partners</h3>
+      <p>
+        We buy the majority of our green coffee through direct relationships rather than on the commodity market.
+        Each year our head roaster travels to at least two origin countries during harvest season, walking the
+        farms, visiting the wet mills, and sitting down with producers to talk about what went well and what
+        didn't. Some of the cooperatives we work with today, like a small women-led group in the Huila region of
+        Colombia, have been selling to us for more than eight years. Those long relationships let farmers plan
+        ahead, invest in better processing equipment, and experiment with new varietals knowing they have a buyer
+        who will take the risk alongside them.
+      </p>
+      <p>
+        When we can't buy directly, we work with a handful of importers who share our standards for transparency.
+        We ask for the farm or washing station name, the elevation, the processing method, and, most importantly,
+        what the producer was actually paid. If an importer can't tell us that, we don't buy from them.
+      </p>
+
+      <h3>Paying Fairly</h3>
+      <p>
+        Coffee prices on the global market swing wildly, and in lean years the commodity price can fall below what
+        it costs a farmer to grow the crop. We think that's unacceptable. Our policy is to pay a floor price that
+        sits well above Fairtrade minimums regardless of where the market is, plus quality premiums for lots that
+        score above 86 points on the Specialty Coffee Association scale. Last year, our average price paid at the
+        farm gate was roughly two and a half times the commodity rate. It makes our coffee a bit more expensive,
+        and we're comfortable explaining why.
+      </p>
+
+      <h3>Cupping and Selection</h3>
+      <p>
+        Before we commit to a lot, we cup it, often several times. Samples arrive at the roastery in small vacuum
+        bags, and we roast them lightly so that nothing masks their natural flavors. Our team tastes each one
+        blind, scoring sweetness, acidity, body, balance, and aftertaste. We're looking for coffees that are clean
+        and expressive: a washed Ethiopian with bright notes of bergamot and jasmine, or a natural-process
+        Guatemalan that tastes like ripe cherries and dark chocolate. Fewer than one in five samples we taste
+        makes the cut.
+      </p>
+
+      <h3>Protecting Freshness in Transit</h3>
+      <p>
+        Green coffee is surprisingly fragile. Heat and humidity during a long sea voyage can flatten its flavor
+        before it ever reaches us. That's why all of our coffee ships in hermetically sealed GrainPro liners
+        inside traditional jute bags, and we time our purchases so beans arrive within a few months of harvest.
+        Once they land, they're stored in a climate-controlled warehouse just a few miles from our roastery and
+        are typically roasted within the year.
+      </p>
+
+      <h3>Sustainability Beyond the Bean</h3>
+      <p>
+        Sourcing responsibly isn't only about price. We prioritize farms that use shade-grown methods, which
+        protect bird habitats and reduce the need for chemical fertilizers. We contribute a portion of every bag
+        sold to a community fund that our partner cooperatives direct themselves; in recent years it has paid for
+        a new water filtration system for a school in Sidama and for drying beds that help farmers in Huehuetenango
+        get a better price for their crop. At home, our spent chaff and burlap sacks go to local gardeners and
+        urban farms instead of the landfill.
+      </p>
+
+      <h3>From Their Hands to Your Cup</h3>
+      <p>
+        By the time a bag of beans reaches our roaster, it has passed through dozens of hands and traveled
+        thousands of miles. We roast in small batches, usually no more than thirty pounds at a time, so we can
+        adjust each profile to bring out the best in that particular lot. Then we get it to our cafés and your
+        door as quickly as possible, because we believe the people who grew this coffee deserve to have it
+        tasted at its peak. The next time you take a sip, we hope you'll taste a little bit of that journey.
+      </p>
+    </article>
+  </section>
